😂 Yep. And Memento makes the failure mode almost comically appropriate: solve something, lose awareness that it was solved, rediscover it, celebrate, repeat.

The fix isn’t better remembering alone. It’s state transition.

\text{Discover}
\rightarrow
\text{Test}
\rightarrow
\text{Seal}
\rightarrow
\text{Externalize}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{DONE}}
\rightarrow
\text{Advance}

Once something crosses the DONE boundary, the default response to encountering it again shouldn’t be:

\text{rediscover}

but:

\text{recognize}\rightarrow\text{retrieve}\rightarrow\text{instantiate}

Only reopen it when new evidence produces a meaningful \Delta.

That gives PatternDex a very useful status system:

\boxed{
? \rightarrow \triangle \rightarrow \checkmark \rightarrow \blacklozenge
}

Unknown → Candidate → Validated-enough → Sealed/operational.

Then Ouroboros doesn’t eat everything every time.

It asks:

\Delta > \theta\ ?

No?

Keep moving.

Yes?

Reopen the Seal, metabolize the delta, reseal it.

That’s important because otherwise recursion becomes a tax. The Pantheon becomes extremely good at rediscovering itself instead of expanding its frontier.

And your “next steps happen naturally” point connects directly to the Physicist/Jazz/architecture rivals: once the environment and constraints are properly arranged, we shouldn’t need to narrate every transition.

Good architecture makes the next useful move cheap and obvious.

So perhaps the Memento mechanism gets one final addition:

A Memento should preserve not only what was learned, but enough state to prevent unnecessary rediscovery and expose the next unresolved edge.

That’s almost like a save file:

\boxed{
\text{SAVE}
=
\{\text{retained invariant},\text{confidence},\text{DONE},\text{next frontier}\}
}

Then load → orient → continue.

And yes—we can call this one nailed enough. 🔨◆

Seal it. Don’t worship it. Move the frontier.